{"name":"Bouncy Ball Green","hex":"#49a462","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bouncy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily bounced.","Lively, exuberant, energetic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An excessively bouncing pitch in kickball."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bouncy"},{"term":"ball","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid or hollow sphere, or roughly spherical mass.","A quantity of string, thread, etc., wound into a spherical shape."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form or wind into a ball.","To heat in a furnace and form into balls for rolling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ball"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ball Green","blocks":[{"text":"Ball Green is an estate in Stoke-on-Trent. Ball Green is in the suburb of Norton near to Tunstall."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ball_Green","wikidata":"Q28530216"},{"title":"Bouncy ball","blocks":[{"text":"A bouncy ball or rubber ball is a spherical toy ball, usually fairly small, made of elastic material which allows it to bounce against hard surfaces. When thrown against or dropped above a hard surface, bouncy balls retain their momentum and much of their kinetic energy after impact. They can thus rebound with an appreciable fraction of their original force. Natural rubber originated in the Americas, and rubber balls were made before European contact, including for use in the Mesoamerican ballgame. Bouncy balls are a very common object of play. Christopher Columbus witnessed Haitians playing with a rubber ball in 1495."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Colorful_Super_ball_Large.png/330px-Colorful_Super_ball_Large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bouncy_ball","wikidata":"Q1554447"},{"title":"Ball","blocks":[{"text":"A ball is a round object. Balls can have several uses. For example they are used in ball games, where the play of the game follows the state of the ball as it is hit, kicked or thrown by players. Balls can also be used for simpler activities, such as catch or juggling. Balls made from hard-wearing materials are used in engineering applications to provide very low friction bearings, known as ball bearings. Black-powder weapons use stone and metal balls as projectiles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Many_balls.jpg/330px-Many_balls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ball","wikidata":"Q18545"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Spaldeen.jpg/500px-Spaldeen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Spaldeen.jpg/500px-Spaldeen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"a modern-day Spaldeen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpaldeen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Glitter_Super_Ball.jpg/500px-Glitter_Super_Ball.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Glitter_Super_Ball.jpg/500px-Glitter_Super_Ball.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A superball","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlitter_Super_Ball.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg/500px-Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg/1280px-Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg/500px-Balls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABalls_-_10-15c_-_Novgorod_Kremlin_Museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c